  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Will Warren shut down Chicago for 6.2 scoreless innings, and the Yankees won the pitching battle.",
    "what_this_means": "Warren's line was the story. He allowed four hits, struck out seven, and gave the offense a real window. The kind of clean, efficient start that should be enough in any game, let alone a 2-0 decision. Two solo home runs decided it. Jones and Rosario each got one swing that mattered, which is all the lineup needed because the pitching never gave ground. Blackburn and Bednar finished the job without a scare. This is what a controlled game looks like from the inside: the starter establishes early, the bullpen holds the door, the bats convert singles into runs when they absolutely have to. It's not flashy. It's not supposed to be. Warren made it impossible for Chicago to get anything started, and nobody else on the roster had to carry a moment they couldn't finish. One run in eight innings should lose almost every game. Two runs, clean pitching, and zero relief damage wins it. They got out of Wrigley without needing a rally.",
    "what_to_watch": "They're now 2.5 back and trending the right way because the foundation is holding. That matters more than the final score right now.",
